In strict SI units

\lambda=\frac{\hbar}{m_\phi c}
=\frac{\hbar c}{m_\phi c^2}
=\frac{(1.054\,571\,817\times10^{-34}\,\text{J·s})(2.997\,924\,58\times10^8\,\text{m/s})}
{(1~\text{meV})(1.602\,176\,634\times10^{-22}\,\text{J/meV})}.
Numerator: \hbar c=3.161\,526\,77\times10^{-26}\ \text{J·m}.
Denominator: 1~\text{meV}=1.602\,176\,634\times10^{-22}\ \text{J}.

\lambda=\frac{3.161\,526\,77\times10^{-26}}{1.602\,176\,634\times10^{-22}}\ \text{m}
=1.973\,269\,8\times10^{-4}\ \text{m}
\approx 197.3~\mu\text{m}.

So m_\phi\simeq 1~\text{meV} corresponds to \lambda\simeq 200~\mu\text{m} (more precisely 197.3~\mu\text{m}).

